# Formatting and lint on staged files only — under a second in practice.
#
# Deliberately not the test suite. The Worker tests boot workerd and the
# simulator sleeps on purpose, so a full run is closer to a minute — and a
# pre-commit hook that slow gets bypassed with --no-verify the first time
# someone is in a hurry. A hook people routinely skip is worse than no hook.
# Correctness is CI's job; this one only keeps mechanical noise out of the diff.
pnpm exec lint-staged

# The leak check, and this is the only layer where it can do its job.
#
# CI runs it too, but CI runs *after* the commit has reached a public
# repository — at which point a leaked word is in the git history, publicly and
# permanently. Reverting does not remove it: GitHub keeps unreachable objects
# fetchable, and any fork keeps its own copy. A CI failure here is a fire alarm
# that rings after the building has burned.
#
# It is also the only layer where the vocabulary half can run at all. The word
# list is gitignored and never published, so it exists only where someone is
# writing — which is here, and not on a runner. See scripts/check-leak.mjs.
#
# Fast enough to survive: ~60ms warm, which is the test the comment above sets.
pnpm check:leak
